import gfx, shell; from shell import fig
G = gfx; F = G.F; METHODS = "https://claude.ai/artifact/HkkFNpzoEPwdLzC7mcPLrZ"
body = f'''
<div class="col">
<p class="kicker">Opinion pitch · mock-up · alternate angle</p>
<h1>New York Ran Eight Experiments on Its Buses This Year. One Kind Worked.</h1>
<p class="dek">A busway, a street closed for the World Cup, new bus lanes, smarter traffic lights, camera fines. We measured each one against every bus in the city. Getting cars out of the way beat everything else by a wide margin.</p>
<p class="byline">Mock-up for a pitch · Analysis of bus position reports, 27 April to 21 November 2026 · Draft text, real data</p>
<p>Every mayor promises faster buses. The tools are always the same: paint a lane, tune a signal, mount a camera, and, once in a while, close a street to cars. What is usually missing is a clean measurement of which of them does anything.</p>
<p>This year supplied one. Between April and November the city switched each of these on somewhere, on a known date, while buses everywhere else carried on as before. We tracked every bus on every route through all of it.</p>
</div>
{fig("What each change did to bus travel time", "Change on the streets where it happened, compared with similar streets over the same weeks. Bars to the left are faster buses.", G.scoreboard(), "Busway: weekdays 18 May to 26 June against 28 September to 20 November, inside the busway limits, net of the change on other Bronx streets. 42nd Street: noon to 8pm on seven match days against 64 ordinary days in the same mix of weekdays, Saturdays and Sundays. Broadway, Queens: the five weeks before the lane went in (week of 1 June) against June to November, Q70 buses eastbound from 69th Street to Roosevelt Avenue; signal priority was added at the same time. Madison and Lexington: against neighbouring blocks of the same avenue; Madison’s “before” is a construction period. Signal priority and cameras: four weeks before and after each start date, against other streets in the same borough; lines show the 95 percent range.")}
<div class="col">
<h2>The busway</h2>
<p>On 19 September, East Tremont Avenue in the Bronx became a busway for half a mile: from 6am to 8pm, cars must turn off at the next corner. Inside it, the Bx36 lost a third less time westbound and a seventh less eastbound. On the blocks either side, nothing changed. Split the delay by cause and the busway did what a busway should: other traffic fell 56 percent westbound and 30 percent eastbound, and westbound even the red lights cost half as much. Time at bus stops barely moved.</p>
</div>
{fig("On East Tremont Avenue, the busway took out the traffic delay and left the stops", "Delay per bus along the street before and after the busway opened on 19 September, split by what causes it.", G.tremont3(), "Above the street: weekdays 18 May to 26 June, before construction. Below it, upside down: 28 September to 20 November, with the earlier curve as a dashed line. The coloured shapes are drawn smoothly and are indicative. Orange is other traffic, blue is bus stops, blue fading to red is a stop just before a light, red is lights. The busway runs eastbound from Third Avenue to Southern Boulevard and westbound from Southern Boulevard to Belmont Avenue, 6am to 8pm.", legend=G.legend(("other", "near", "stop", "lights")))}
<div class="col">
<p>A smaller version of the same idea went in on Broadway in Queens in the first week of June: a centre lane for the Q70 to LaGuardia, eastbound from 69th Street to Roosevelt Avenue. In the evening rush the Q70 had been crossing that stretch at 4.3 miles an hour. From June on it ran at 5.1, and has held there since.</p>
<h2>The accidental busway</h2>
<p>On eight days in June and July, for World Cup matches across the river, the city closed 42nd Street to everything but buses from six hours before kickoff. Nobody called it a transit project. The M42, named the city’s slowest route again this month, ran about 20 percent faster while the street was closed. Before the barriers went up each morning, it ran exactly as slowly as usual.</p>
</div>
{fig("With cars off 42nd Street, the M42 sped up, and only then", "Average speed of the M42 along 42nd Street, by hour, on seven World Cup match days and on ordinary days.", G.worldcup_all(), "Match days: three weekdays, two Saturdays and two Sundays. The eighth, a Monday with an 8pm kickoff, is left out because its closure began only at 2pm. Ordinary days: 64 days from 18 May to 31 July, with weekdays, Saturdays and Sundays averaged in the same three-two-two mix. Grey band: the usual day-to-day range (one standard deviation). Faint lines are the seven match days, smoothed. The closures began between 8am and noon. Over the same hours, buses on 34th, 50th and 57th Streets ran 3 to 11 percent slower than usual.")}
<div class="col">
<p>Laid out along the street, the match days look like the busway in the Bronx. Over the whole day, other traffic cost a bus a quarter less eastbound and more than a third less westbound. Red lights cost less too. Time at bus stops did not fall.</p>
</div>
{fig("42nd Street with and without cars", "Delay per bus along 42nd Street on ordinary days (above the street) and on seven World Cup match days (below, upside down), split by what causes it.", G.m42(), "All M42 trips on each day, whole day, so the hours before the closure are included and the effect during the closure is larger. Seven match days (three weekdays, two Saturdays, two Sundays; about 700 buses each way) against 60 ordinary days from mid-May to July, weighted to the same mix of weekdays, Saturdays and Sundays. With this few buses the split by cause is rough; the shapes are indicative. An MTA service alert shows the M42’s stops at First and Second Avenues were closed during the World Cup, which would trim stop delay at the eastern end.", legend=G.legend(("other", "near", "stop", "lights")))}
<div class="col">
<p>Could it be the days and not the street? The M42 never leaves 42nd Street, so it has no untouched stretch to compare. Two other checks say no. All seven match days were faster than usual, and five were faster than almost every ordinary day. And the Staten Island express buses that use 42nd Street for part of their trip sped up 17 percent on it and only 5 percent on their other Manhattan streets, on the same days.</p>
</div>
{fig("Two checks that it was the street, not the days", "Left: each day’s M42 travel time on 42nd Street. Right: five express routes, on 42nd Street and off it.", G.m42check(), "Whole-day travel time per mile, 18 May to 31 July. Drawing seven ordinary days at random 20,000 times, with the same mix of weekdays, Saturdays and Sundays, never produced a set as fast as the match days: the fastest draw was 6 percent quicker than usual, the match days 11 percent. Express routes run on weekdays only, so their comparison rests on the three weekday matches; some of their other streets were also restricted on match days.")}
<div class="col">
<p>The permanent version is a mile and a half south. Since 2019, 14th Street has been closed to through traffic. Of all Manhattan’s crosstown streets it now loses the least time to other traffic: about a third as much as 34th, 42nd or 57th.</p>
</div>
{fig("14th Street loses a third as much time to traffic as its neighbours", "Minutes a bus needs to cover a mile on Manhattan’s crosstown streets, and what the time is made of.", G.crosstown(), "Both directions, all routes on the street, all days. Bus stops include stops placed just before a light. A comparison across streets, not a before-and-after.", legend=G.legend(("ff", "stop", "lights", "other")))}
<div class="col">
<p>Look at what is left on 14th Street, though. Even with the traffic gone, a bus needs more than ten minutes a mile, because stops and red lights are untouched. A busway fixes the orange. It does not fix the blue or the red.</p>
<h2>The lights</h2>
<p>For the red there is signal priority: a light that sees the bus coming and holds its green. The city turned it on at more than 280 intersections during these months. At the average light it saved a bus 0.7 seconds.</p>
</div>
{fig("Signal priority: three lights where it worked, and all the others", "Seconds a bus loses at the light, week by week, and the change at every light where priority was switched on.", G.tsp(), "Top: weekly averages on weekdays; horizontal lines are the averages before and after. Bottom: each dot is one approach to a light, four weeks after against four weeks before, net of the change at other lights in the same borough. The black line is the average.")}
{fig("The five lights where priority did the most", "Delay per bus approaching each light, four weeks before priority was switched on (dashed) and four weeks after (filled).", G.tsp5s(), "The five approaches with the largest drop in delay at the light, net of the change at other lights in the same borough. Red is the delay credited to the light, blue to a bus stop, orange is other traffic; the shapes are drawn smoothly and are indicative. Two were left out: Dyer Avenue at 41st Street, on the Lincoln Tunnel approach, where delay swings widely from week to week, and one with too few buses after the switch. Picking the best five overstates what a typical light gains.", legend=G.legend(("lights", "stop", "near", "other")))}
<div class="col">
<p>These intersections show what the technology can do: up to a fifth of the wait, gone in a week and gone for good. The cloud of dots shows what it usually does. And where a bus stop sits just before the light, priority did nothing at all, because a bus that is loading passengers cannot use a green.</p>
<p>That points to the cheapest fix on this page. New York puts the stop before the light more than 3,300 times.</p>
</div>
{fig("A stop before the light costs more than a stop after it", "Average delay around an intersection, for buses going straight through. The light is at the centre of each panel.", G.placement3(), "Only the delay credited to the light and the stop is shown; other traffic is removed. Averages over intersections with at least 350 feet of clear street before and 300 after, buses going straight through. Seconds are per 100 feet. With a far-side stop, delay before the light is counted as the light’s and delay after it as the stop’s. Stops are not placed at random, so part of the gap may reflect busier corners. Counting all time above a clear run within 250 feet, traffic included, gives the same order: 30, 39 and 43 seconds.", legend=G.legend(("lights", "stop", "near")))}
<div class="col">
<h2>The cameras</h2>
<p>The MTA says its bus-mounted cameras, which ticket cars in bus lanes and at bus stops, speed routes up by 5 percent. Fines began on 22 more routes this year, on six different dates. We compared each group with routes not yet fined. Travel time did not fall. If anything it rose by a fraction of a percent, well inside the noise.</p>
<p>Cameras may do other things worth having: fewer blocked stops, fewer crashes. On most of these routes there is no bus lane to protect. But as a way to make buses faster, we could not find them in the data.</p>
<h2>What follows</h2>
<p class="pull">The measures that are easy to announce barely register. The one that takes road space from cars works everywhere it has been tried.</p>
<p>The city’s new bus plan promises a 20 percent gain on 50 corridors. On this year’s evidence there are two ways to get there. Build busways, not painted lanes. And move the stops across the intersection so that the lights can finally help.</p>
<div class="box"><h3>How we did this</h3>
<p>Every MTA bus reports its position about every 30 seconds. We rebuilt each trip’s path along its route for 209 days and averaged the time spent on each 10 feet of street. Subtracting the time a bus takes late at night with nothing in the way leaves the delay; peaks at a stop or light are credited to it, and the smooth level underneath the peaks is other traffic. The full method is in <a href="{METHODS}">Where NYC Buses Lose Time</a>.</p>
<p><b>Limits.</b> None of these was a randomised trial. The busway comparison spans spring to autumn, with other Bronx streets as the control; of 256 half-mile stretches of Bronx bus street, the busway was the single most improved. Match days bring their own crowds. Madison Avenue’s “before” is a construction period. Camera routes share streets with routes that already had cameras, which would mute any effect; restricting to streets served only by the newly fined routes gives the same answer.</p></div>
</div>
'''
open(shell.HERE / "pitch_b.html", "w").write(shell.page("What Actually Sped Up Buses", body)); print(len(body) // 1024, "KB")
